To output a PDF in SAS with a journal format, you can use the `ods` statement with the `pdf` destination and specify the journal format using the `ods` statement with the `layout` option. Here are a few examples:

**Example 1: Simple Journal Format**
```
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
This will output a PDF file named `output.pdf` with a simple journal format.

**Example 2: Customized Journal Format**
```
ods pdf file="output.pdf";
ods layout;
ods layout begin;
ods layout htitle 'Journal of Marketing';
ods layout body begin;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
ods layout body end;
ods layout end;
ods pdf close;
```
This will output a PDF file named `output.pdf` with a customized journal format that includes a header title and a page body.

**Example 3: Using the `ods` `journal` Style**
```
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
ods journal journal=marketing_type;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
run;
ods journal close;
ods pdf close;
```
This will output a PDF file named `output.pdf` with a journal format style specific to the `journal=marketing_type` journal definition.

You can customize the journal format by specifying the following options:

* `title` - sets the title of the journal
* `htitle` - sets the header title of the journal
* `header` - sets the header text of the journal
* `footer` - sets the footer text of the journal
* `body` - sets the body text of the journal
* `layout` - specifies the layout of the journal

You can also use the `ods` statement with the `pdf` destination to output a PDF file with a specific page size, orientation, and margins.

